.TH MODBUS-FAKE-INVERTER 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-fake-inverter \- simulated PV inverter for testing the pv-suite
stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-fake-inverter
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B modbus-fake-inverter \-\-static
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
A stdlib-only Modbus TCP server that plays an inverter \(em with a
battery, a smart meter, the modbus-cache diagnostics block and the
pv-suite's register-map profiles \(em so the scraper, the cache and the
whole stack can be exercised without hardware. The default profile is the
Huawei SUN2000 map; Deye hybrids are included. This is a test instrument:
synthetic data only, no state worth protecting beyond its LAN segment.
.PP
By default it simulates a sunny day: a double-shouldered PV curve over
two MPPT strings (east/west), a home load with morning/evening peaks, a
battery state machine (95 % charge efficiency, self-discharge, an 80 %
discharge cap, temperature derating), per-phase grid voltages and load
shares (mean-reverting wanderers), a grid-frequency machine, and a grid
outage machine (random blackouts). All machines integrate with a simulated
clock \(em UTC by default, freezable (\fB--time\fR) and scalable
(\fB--time-scale\fR for time-lapse). \fB--static\fR replaces the day with
constant demo values. Writes (FC06/FC16) land in an overlay the scrapers
read; \fB--chaos\fR answers a fraction of requests hostile.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-host
bind address (default 127.0.0.1)
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
Modbus TCP port (default 5502)
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " N"
Modbus unit id served (default 1)
.TP
.B \-\-profile
inverter profile JSON: name \-> register (+ optional type/scale), e.g.
profiles/profiles/sun2000.json (the default); PROFILE when unset
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-kwp " N"
PV array size in kWp, peak = 75%% of it (default 6.0)
.TP
.B \-\-timezone
clock the state machines integrate over (the PV bell, the home load, the
clouds, the battery, the grid voltage): UTC (the default) or the local
wall clock (default: utc)
.TP
.B \-\-time
freeze simulated time HH:MM (default: real clock)
.TP
.BI \-\-time\-scale " N"
the simulated clock speed: N times real time \- every machine (the
battery, the clouds, the voltages, the day counters) integrates over it,
so a full simulated day takes 24/N real hours (default: 1 = real time)
(default: 1.0)
.TP
.B \-\-static
constant demo values instead of the day simulation (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-battery
simulate a battery (the default) (default: on)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-battery
no battery values (default: on)
.TP
.BI \-\-battery\-kwh " N"
usable battery capacity in kWh (default 6.9)
.TP
.B \-\-strict
reject reads of undefined registers with exception 02 (like the real
device) (default: off)
.TP
.BI \-\-chaos " N"
probability 0..1 that a request is answered hostile and dropped
(default: off); seed with \-\-seed for reproducibility (default: 0.0)
.TP
.BI \-\-grid\-voltage " N"
nominal grid phase voltage in V (120 or 230; the per\-phase machine
wanders ±10 %% around it, default 230.0)
.TP
.BI \-\-grid\-frequency " N"
nominal grid frequency in Hz (the machine wanders ±0.2 Hz around it,
default 50.0)
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
chaos RNG seed (default: random)
.TP
.B \-\-connect\-acl
the Modbus clients allowed to connect (the cache's connect\-acl,
mirrored): "any" (the default — the trusted\-LAN posture), "none", or a
comma\-separated list of IPs and CIDR prefixes, e.g.
127.0.0.1,10.48.0.0/16 (env CONNECT_ACL)
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " N"
refuse further Modbus connections past this many simultaneous clients, 0
= no limit (default 64)
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-timeout " N"
disconnect Modbus clients silent for this many seconds, 0 = never
(default 300.0) — sheds connections from clients that died without
closing
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
refuse further Modbus connections from one peer IP past this many
simultaneous connections, 0 = no per\-IP limit (default 0)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-port
control server target: unix socket path (default:
/tmp/fake\-inverter\-<modbus\-port>.socket) or TCP port number on
127.0.0.1 (e.g. 15599); 0 = off. Talk to it with modbus\-fake\-ctl.py
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token
require a token on control connections: the client authenticates with
{"cmd": "auth", "token": ...}; over the unix socket a trusted peer
(root, the daemon's own user or \-\-control\-trust\-uids — SO_PEERCRED)
skips the token (default: $CONTROL_TOKEN or none)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token\-file
the token from a file (the first line; e.g. a systemd LoadCredential or
a key file; must be owner\-only 0600) — takes the place of
\-\-control\-token; giving both is an error (env CONTROL_TOKEN_FILE)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token
a second, read\-only control token: sessions authenticating with it may
run the show/clear verbs only (no set/unset, no reset, no shutdown) —
the ops shift against the admin key, both audited (env
CONTROL_OPS_TOKEN)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token\-file
the read\-only ops token from a file (the first line; must be
owner\-only 0600) — takes the place of \-\-control\-ops\-token; giving
both is an error (env CONTROL_OPS_TOKEN_FILE)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-verbs
the read\-only ops role's RBAC policy: the verbs its sessions may run,
comma\-separated (default: show,clear; validated against the control
grammar at start; env CONTROL_OPS_VERBS)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-trust\-uids
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token
(SO_PEERCRED — a local systemd unit speaks without the secret); root and
the daemon's own user are always trusted (env CONTROL_TRUST_UIDS)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-socket\-group
leave the unix control socket group\-accessible (chmod 0660 instead of
the default 0600): a local group may speak to the control without being
the service user (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-log\-replies
DEBUG\-log the full output of every control command (the default logs
the command only) (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-server
serve the fake's own Prometheus /metrics endpoint (the live `set
metrics\-server` toggle exists too); off by default (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-listen
the metrics bind address HOST:PORT (default 127.0.0.1:9452)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-token
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on the /metrics endpoint (the same
scheme the scraper speaks)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-token\-file
the metrics bearer token from a file (the first line; must be
owner\-only 0600) — takes the place of \-\-metrics\-token; giving both
is an error (env METRICS_TOKEN_FILE)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-clients " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP clients on the /metrics endpoint; extras get an
immediate 503, 0 = unlimited (default 64)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-client\-timeout " N"
seconds an idle /metrics client is kept before the socket times out, 0 =
the built\-in 5 s cap (default 5.0)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
maximum concurrent /metrics connections from one peer IP, 0 = no per\-IP
limit (default 0)
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-log
INV audit trail: a file path or \- for stdout (the docker/journald way);
default: ~/.local/state/fake\-inverter/audit.log — the XDG state home
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-max\-mb " N"
rotate the audit log at this size in MB, keeping 3 backups; 0 = no size
rotation (default 10.0)
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-daily
also rotate the audit log at UTC midnight (default: off)
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-files " N"
rotated audit backups to keep (default 3)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit\-log
disable the INV audit trail (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level
 (default: INFO)
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-path
the state dump: the live setting overrides (the pins, the machines, the
tokens apart) as one JSON, written 0600 with an integrity hash (a
restore refuses a tampered, unsigned or group\-writable dump); dumped on
the request dump command and the graceful shutdown; loaded on start only
with \-\-restore\-on\-start, on demand with the request restore command
(default: $DUMP_PATH or none)
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-key
the key that seals the state dumps and the reload's state file: the
state rides encrypted (an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode keystream,
authenticated — a restore refuses a wrong or missing key, and a
configured key refuses an unsealed dump); the reload re\-exec
re\-resolves the same key, so the state file reads on the other side; no
key = the dumps stay 0600 plaintext with the integrity hash (default:
$DUMP_KEY or none)
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-on\-start
load the state dump when the fake starts (needs \-\-dump\-path) — the
live overrides ride again instead of a cold start; default: start cold,
load only on demand (default: $RESTORE_ON_START) (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
validate the configuration, print the effective settings and exit
(nothing is bound or connected) (default: off)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
re-apply the environment layer (all-or-nothing).
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
reset the scenario: runtime pins, the cloud machine and the write overlay
are dropped.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown with a session summary.

.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines
(\fB{"cmd": "..."}\fR -> \fB{"status": "ok|err|bye", "text": "..."}\fR;
TCP requires the token when configured). Commands: \fBset\fR/\fBunset\fR/
\fBshow\fR/\fBhelp\fR (the settings: pv, load, batt, grid, grid-voltage,
grid-frequency, grid-outage, soc, mppt, cloud, timezone, time, time-scale,
scenario, chaos, seed, battery, battery-state, strict, static, max-kwp,
battery-kwh, max-clients, client-timeout, max-conn-per-ip, metrics-server,
register, profile, log-level \(em and the token lives: control token,
the ops token, metrics token, each rotating live, the value never
rendering \(em \fBshow <secret>\fR refuses),
\fBshow status\fR, \fBshow connections\fR (the live Modbus clients: peer,
age, requests), \fBshow registers\fR,
\fBshow version\fR, \fBshow knobs\fR (the dictionary of the registry),
\fBinspect\fR, \fBreset\fR, \fBreload\fR (the whole-daemon restart
that keeps the pins: the overrides save to a state file in the XDG state
home \(em the fake has no config file \(em, the process re-execs, the
fresh fake re-applies them once), with a dump path configured
(\fB--dump-path\fR): \fBrequest dump\fR / \fBrequest restore\fR (the
overrides as one 0600 integrity-hashed JSON; \fB--dump-key\fR seals it
\(em the state rides encrypted, a restore refuses a wrong or missing key;
\fB--restore-on-start\fR
loads it at start), \fBshutdown\fR,
\fBquit\fR. A second, read-only token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR)
authenticates into the show verbs only \(em the ops shift against the
admin key, both roles audited. See
.BR modbus-fake-ctl (1)
and
.BR modbus-fake-cli (1).

.SH SECURITY
The fake is a development/test instrument, not a security-first design
(the audit's F11): the Modbus surface speaks to any peer that can reach
the port \(em no TLS, no auth, no ACL \(em and the metrics endpoint
serves without a token unless one is configured. Run it on a trusted,
LAN-isolated network, next to the tools it is meant to serve.
\fB--max-clients\fR/\fB--client-timeout\fR cap runaway connections and
the read-count gate keeps a malformed frame from assembling an oversized
reply. The control surface can still carry the family's read-only ops
token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR) when the ops shift only ever watches.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHAOS ", " MAX_KWP ", " PROFILE ", " BATTERY ,
.B STRICT ", " TIME ", " TIME_SCALE ", " LOG_LEVEL
the SIGHUP-reloadable layer.
.TP
.B CTL
the default control target for the clients.
.TP
.B CONTROL_TOKEN ", " CLI_KEEPALIVE
the clients' TCP token and idle-ping interval.
.TP
.B CONTROL_OPS_TOKEN ", " CONTROL_OPS_TOKEN_FILE
the daemon's read-only ops token (the flagless start; the OPTIONS above).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/fake-inverter-<port>.socket
the default control socket.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/fake-inverter/audit.log
the default audit trail.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-fake-ctl (1),
.BR modbus-fake-cli (1),
.BR modbus-cache (1),
.BR modbus-scraper (1)
.PP
\fBinverter/README.md\fR in the suite checkout \(em the full documentation,
including the state machines.
